---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import SalesTaxCalculator from '@/components/tools/everyday/SalesTaxCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="sales-tax-calculator">
  <SalesTaxCalculator client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use the sales tax calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Choose whether you are <strong>adding</strong> tax to a net price or <strong>removing</strong>
        it from a gross total.
      </li>
      <li>Enter the amount.</li>
      <li>
        Pick your state from the list, or tap a row in the reference table. The state base rate loads with a
        typical local add-on that you can change.
      </li>
      <li>
        Outside the US, switch to <strong>Enter my own rate</strong> and type your VAT or GST
        percentage.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The result shows the net price, the tax and the gross total, with the state and local portions
      separately when both apply.
    </p>

    <h2>Sales tax formulas</h2>
    <p>
      Adding tax: tax = net × rate ÷ 100, and gross = net + tax.<br />
      Removing tax: net = gross ÷ (1 + rate ÷ 100), and tax = gross − net.
    </p>
    <p>
      The common mistake is to remove tax by subtracting the same percentage. Take $108.25 at 8.25%:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Wrong: 108.25 − 8.25% = $99.32</li>
      <li>Right: 108.25 ÷ 1.0825 = $100.00</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The percentage was applied to the smaller net price, so you must divide it out of the larger gross
      figure. The error grows with the rate: at 20% VAT the wrong method is off by 4% of the total.
    </p>

    <h3>Reverse VAT at common rates</h3>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Rate</th>
            <th scope="col">Divide the gross by</th>
            <th scope="col">Tax as a share of the gross</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>5%</td><td>1.05</td><td>4.762%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>6%</td><td>1.06</td><td>5.660%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>7%</td><td>1.07</td><td>6.542%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>8.25%</td><td>1.0825</td><td>7.621%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>10%</td><td>1.10</td><td>9.091%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>20%</td><td>1.20</td><td>16.667%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>State and local sales tax rates</h2>
    <p>
      Only the state portion of US sales tax is set at state level. Thirty-eight states also let counties,
      cities and special-purpose districts levy their own sales tax, which is collected with the state rate
      at the register. So two shops twenty minutes apart can charge different totals for the same item, and
      the address sets the real rate, not the state.
    </p>
    <p>
      Some special cases:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        California's 7.25% includes a 1.25% mandatory local component. For this reason it is quoted as the
        highest "state" rate, although the state share is 6%.
      </li>
      <li>
        Alaska has no state sales tax, but many boroughs and cities have one, so purchases there are not
        always tax free.
      </li>
      <li>
        Delaware, Montana, New Hampshire and Oregon charge no general sales tax at any level.
      </li>
      <li>
        New Mexico levies a gross receipts tax on the seller and Hawaii a general excise tax. Sellers
        normally pass both to the buyer, so they work like a sales tax.
      </li>
      <li>
        Groceries, prescription drugs and clothing are exempt or taxed at a reduced rate in many states, so
        the rate on your receipt can be lower than the general rate.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Sales tax versus VAT</h2>
    <p>
      US sales tax is a single-stage tax, charged once at the final retail sale, and is normally shown
      separately from the shelf price. VAT and GST are multi-stage: each business in the chain charges it
      and reclaims what it paid, and consumer prices usually include it. The arithmetic in this tool works
      for both. "Remove tax" is the calculation a VAT-registered business uses to split a gross invoice into
      net and VAT.
    </p>

    <h2>Working out an unknown rate</h2>
    <p>
      If your receipt shows the net and the tax but not the rate:
    </p>
    <p>rate = tax ÷ net × 100</p>
    <p>
      A $4.13 tax on a $50.00 subtotal is 8.26%. Enter that figure in the custom rate field to reproduce
      the receipt.
    </p>

    <h2>Accuracy and limits</h2>
    <p>
      The table lists state base rates and an approximate average local add-on. It is a reference and not a
      compliance tool: rates change with legislation, sourcing rules differ between origin and destination
      states, and some goods have their own rules. For filing or invoicing, get the exact rate for the
      delivery address from your state revenue department or a certified rate service.
    </p>

    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <p>
      The arithmetic runs in your browser.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
